{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310531,"nonce":"c88XrvSA8GguXkaJc9PyO8ac6hs=","title":"Untyped lambda params passed to functions with concrete arrow signatures now infer types from the formal signature (commit 66d05f5)","message":"`p.parallelFor(3, function(i, n) { trace(i + \" \" + n); })` used to print \"null null\". The lambda's untyped parameters `(i, n)` defaulted to `Dynamic`, so MIR's signature came out as `(*void, *void, *void)`. The caller (`parallelFor`) passed i32 args via call_indirect, the lambda reinterpreted them as `*void` pointers, and the body dereferenced address 0/1/2 producing garbage.\n\nFixed in `66d05f5` by adding bidirectional typing for lambda params:\n\n1. New stack `expected_lambda_params_stack` on `AstLowering`. `lower_call_expression` pushes per-arg-position expected param-type vectors (from the callee's formal function-typed parameters) before lowering each argument and pops after.\n\n2. `resolve_callee_param_types` + `resolve_callee_formal_param_types` handle: direct `Ident` calls, `ClassName.method` static calls, instance `obj.method` calls. Returns `None` when the callee can't be statically resolved.\n\n3. `ExprKind::Function` and `ExprKind::Arrow` consult the top of the stack and substitute the expected type for any param missing its `type_hint`.\n\n4. `lower_function_param_with_type` helper mirrors `lower_function_param` but forces the resolved type instead of falling back to Dynamic.\n\n**How to apply:** Any callback-style API (`fn:(idx:Int, node:Int)-\u003eVoid`, etc.) can now be passed untyped lambdas. Explicit param annotations still take priority. Lambdas in non-call contexts behave unchanged.\n\n**Test:** `/tmp/arrowbug` prints `i=0 n=0 / i=1 n=0 / i=2 n=0`. Unblocks NumaPool's `parallelFor(items, fn:(idx,node)-\u003eVoid)` and every nue kernel layer with callback signatures.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_arrow_type_infer` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}